KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ float declaration as if condition: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ zero is false: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
unexpected expression
--
N5008 [stmt.select.general]/4 + [conv.bool]: a condition that is a
declaration `if(float a = x)' tests the declared object converted to bool,
i.e. `a != 0'.  For a floating-point object the front end builds an
`ieee_float_notequal' node that the statement type checker does not accept
("unexpected expression: ieee_float_notequal") and the enclosing function
is rejected -- as a plain `if(x)' on a float works, the fault is in the
declaration form.  libstdc++'s three-argument std::hypot (`__hypot3', `if
(_Tp __a = ...)') is dropped by it (audit residue item 8, round 162).
